ize each call, branch; using local pointers in nextInt can reduce. Let's rewrite scanner for speed.\n\nPotential issue: current `nextInt` consumes one delimiter after number (the whitespace) by reading it and not storing; that's okay but means when parsing next int starts after delimiter already consumed? Actually while digit loop reads c=getChar() until non-digit, so the non-digit is consumed. Then next call do-while skips further whitespace. Fine.\n\nImplement:\n```\nclass FastScanner {\n static constexpr int BUFSIZE=1<<20;\n char* cur; char* end; char buf[BUFSIZE];\n bool fill(){ size_t r=fread(buf,1,BUFSIZE,stdin); cur=buf; end=buf+r; return r>0;}\npublic:\n FastScanner():cur(buf),end(buf){}\n int nextInt(){\n   while(cur==end && !fill()) return 0;\n   char c=*cur++;\n   while(c<=' '){ if(cur==end&&!fill())return 0; c=*cur++; }\n   int sign=1; if(c=='-'){sign=-1; if(cur==end&&!fill())return 0; c=*cur++;}\n   int x=c-'0';\n   for(;;){\n     if(cur==end && !fill()) break;\n     char d=*cur++;\n     if(d<'0'||d>'9') break;\n     x=x*10 + (d-'0');\n   }\n   return sign*x;\n }\n};\n```\nNeed handle EOF carefully. Input valid, no need robust? But correctness all sizes; can assume well-formed. Simpler:\n```\nint nextInt() {\n  while (*cur <= ' ') { ++cur; if(cur==end) refill();}\n...\n}\n```\nBut checking cur==end each char costly.\n\nCould use `fread` with large buffer and parse using pointer, with a helper to ensure at least one byte. Since input size maybe up to ~100MB (8000^2*11=704MB? Actually 64M ints * avg 10 =640MB), BUFSIZE 1<<20 okay.\n\nMaybe use `read` syscall directly into buffer and parse; fread overhead minimal.\n\nCould use `scanf(\"%d\")` no, slower.\n\nLet's focus on algorithmic constant: multi-window horizontal scan + incremental ring pointers + unrolled update. Also maybe avoid storing col as vector<long long> initialized to zero with calloc? vector does value-initialize zeros O(n), fine.\n\nPotential memory allocation of ring `new int[ringSize*n]` may be huge and cause page faults; but necessary. Could use `malloc` no difference.\n\nCould reduce ring size from s+1 to s? Need store S rows for vertical window: when adding new row i+S-1, need subtract outgoing row i-1. At that moment we have stored rows i..i+S-2 (S-1 rows) plus incoming; total S rows needed before overwriting oldest? Let's simulate ring size S+1 current uses slots 0..s for s+1 rows including one extra. Could use exactly S slots with careful timing: Initially store rows 0..S-1 in slots 0..S-1 (S rows). For new row index S, need subtract outgoing row 0 and can overwrite slot 0 after reading? But we need old row 0 values to compute deltas; if we read incoming into same slot before using pout, lose. Could use temporary buffer for incoming then write over outgoing after computing delta. That would require temp n ints (32KB) but reduce ring memory by factor S/(S+1), negligible and maybe improve cache? Current ring has one extra row of n ints; not significant.\n\nCould avoid storing full rows by storing column values in transposed layout to make vertical update more cache-friendly? Current reads input row-major, so storing row contiguous is natural. Update col[j] accesses pout[j], pin[j], c[j] all sequential across j, good.\n\nMaybe use `#pragma omp parallel for` on horizontal scan and col update with static scheduling; but per-row overhead maybe high. Could parallelize over columns within each row using OpenMP if enabled? Need know Makefile may not include -fopenmp. We cannot change Makefile. Source can use `<omp.h>` only if compiled with -fopenmp; unknown. Avoid.\n\nCould use std::thread? Overhead worse and need link pthread maybe not.\n\nLet's inspect baseline/parent times: parent .01167 sec for 3 samples, likely N small (maybe 1000). If N=8000, reading 64M ints alone would take >0.1s; so benchmark input probably moderate. Optimizations may still help.\n\nNeed produce one candidate with complete source file. We can include multiple transformations in `transformations` list and notes.\n\nLet's write optimized solution.cpp carefully preserving correctness.\n\nPlan:\n\n1. FastScanner improved:\n- Use local pointers cur/end, buffer size 1<<20.\n- nextInt inline with minimal checks; assume valid input no EOF during number? But need handle final newline. We'll implement robust enough.\n\nImplementation details for scanner:\n```\nclass FastScanner {\n    static constexpr int BUFSIZE = 1 << 16? maybe 1<<20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    char* cur;\n    char* end;\n    bool refill() {\n        size_t r = fread(buf,1,BUFSIZE,stdin);\n        cur=buf; end=buf+r;\n        return r>0;\n    }\npublic:\n    FastScanner():cur(buf),end(buf){}\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        while (true) {\n            if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n            char c = *cur++;\n            if (c > ' ') {\n                int sign=1;\n                if(c=='-'){sign=-1; if(cur==end&&!refill())return 0; c=*cur++;}\n                int x=c-'0';\n                while(true){\n                    if(cur==end && !refill()) break;\n                    char d=*cur++;\n                    if(d<'0'||d>'9') break;\n                    x=x*10 + (d-'0');\n                }\n                return sign*x;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n};\n```\nBut after reading digit loop, when it breaks on non-digit, that delimiter is consumed. Good.\nPotential issue: If refill occurs exactly at end of buffer and next char is whitespace, okay.\n\nCould optimize by not checking `cur==end` inside digit loop every time? Need handle buffer boundary; input large so frequent. Could use a sentinel? Not easy with fread.\n\nMaybe use larger BUFSIZE 1<<20 to reduce refills.\n\n2. Main:\n```\nint n=fs.nextInt();\nint s=fs.nextInt();\nconst size_t ringSize=(size_t)s+1;\nstd::unique_ptr<int[]> storage(new int[ringSize*n]);\nint* __restrict ring = storage.get(); // restrict not allowed on local? `int* __restrict ring` GCC extension.\nstd::vector<long long> col(n,0);\nlong long* __restrict c=col.data();\n```\nNeed include `<cstdlib>` for aligned alloc? no.\n\nInitial read:\n```\nfor(int r=0;r<s;++r){\n  int* row = ring + (size_t)r*n;\n  for(int j=0;j<n;++j){ int v=fs.nextInt(); row[j]=v; c[j]+=v; }\n}\n```\n\nCompute initial best using multi-window horizontal max function. We can write a helper inline lambda? To avoid code duplication, define static inline function:\n```\nstatic inline long long scanMax(const long long* __restrict col, int n, int s) { ... }\n```\nBut `__restrict` in parameter okay GCC.\n\nFunction:\n```\nstatic inline long long horizontal_max(const long long* c, int n, int s) {\n    int limit = n - s;\n    if (limit < 0) return LLONG_MIN; // not happen\n    long long w[4];\n    // compute w0\n    long long sum=0;\n    for(int j=0;j<s;++j) sum += c[j];\n    w[0]=sum;\n    int cnt = limit+1; // number of windows\n    if(cnt>1){ w[1] = w[0] - c[0] + c[s]; }\n    if(cnt>2){ w[2] = w[1] - c[1] + c[s+1]; } // wait recurrence: W[j+1]=W[j]-c[j]+c[j+s]. So W[2]=W[1]-c[1]+c[s+1].\n...\n```\nNeed handle s maybe 0? Problem says 0<S<N, so s>=1. Good.\nThen best = max of first up to4.\n\nLoop:\n```\nint j=0;\nfor (; j + 8 <= limit; j += 8) {\n   // current windows at positions j..j+3? We need update from base j to j+4 etc.\n}\n```\nLet's design generic with arrays and unroll by 4:\n\nInitialize w[0],w[1],w[2],w[3] for indices 0..min(3,limit).\nSet best = max(w[t]) for t<numInit.\n\nThen process groups:\nFor base=0; base+4<=limit; base+=4:\n   // We have current windows at positions base,base+1,base+2,base+3 (because initialized and maintained)\n   // Compute next four at base+4..base+7:\n   w[0] += c[base+s] - c[base];\n   w[1] += c[base+1+s] - c[base+1];\n   w[2] += c[base+2+s] - c[base+2];\n   w[3] += c[base+3+s] - c[base+3];\n   // update best with these\n```\nBut after first iteration base=0, we compute windows 4..7. Then next base should be 4; loop increments by4 and repeats using same w values now representing positions 4..7? Wait at start of each iteration, invariant: w[t] = W[base+t]. Initially base=0 yes. After updating to base+4+t, set base +=4, so invariant holds. Good.\nNeed ensure when computing `c[base+3+s]`, index <= n-1? For window position base+3 (<=limit), highest col index = base+3 + s -1 <= limit+s-1 = n-s +s -1 = n-1. In update formula uses c[base+t+s], where t=0..3, max index = base+3+s. Since base+3 <= limit => base+3+s <= n? Wait W[j] sum indices j..j+s-1; recurrence to get W[j+4] from W[j]: add col[j+s] through col[j+4+s-1], subtract col[j]..col[j+3]. Formula for w[t] update uses c[base+t+s] - c[base+t]; max index base+3+s. If base+3 <= limit = n-s, then base+3+s <= n. But valid col indices 0..n-1; if equals n? Let's check: W[limit]=W[n-s] uses col up to n-1. To update from W[base+t] to W[base+t+4], we need add c[(base+t)+s] ... Actually recurrence one step: W[j+1] = W[j] + c[j+s] - c[j]. For j=base+t, max index j+s = base+t+s. If j <= limit-? We are computing next window at j+4; for t=3, j=base+3. Need ensure j < n-s (limit) because we're updating to W[j+1] etc within loop condition base+4<=limit means base+3 <= limit-1? Let's see: if base+4 <= limit, then max current index = base+3 <= limit-1. So j=base+t <= limit-1 for t<=3. Then c[j+s] max index = (limit-1)+s = n-s-1+s = n-1 valid. Good. After update we have windows up to base+7; loop condition ensures next iteration? At end of iteration, new current indices are base+4..base+7. We then set base +=4. The invariant for next iteration requires those <= limit? Since original base+4<=limit => new max = old base+7 may exceed limit! Wait if we update all four and compare, some computed windows beyond limit should not be considered or used in next iteration. Example limit=5 (windows 0..5), initial w[0..3]. Loop condition base+4<=limit: base=0 => 4<=5 true; compute windows 4..7, but only window 4 and5 valid; w2,w3 invalid (indices6,7). Then best update includes invalid values! Need handle.\n\nBetter process groups such that we don't overshoot. We can initialize first four then loop while base+8 <= limit? Let's think.\n\nWe want compute all windows sequentially but with 4 independent accumulators starting at offsets 0..3 and stepping by4:\n- Accumulator a_t tracks W[base + t] for current base multiple of4.\nAt each step, update to next group base+4+t. If some indices exceed limit, stop before updating those.\n\nSimpler: Use unrolled loop with condition `j + 7 <= limit`? Let's derive safe block:\nWe can process windows in chunks of 4 starting at j where we have current W[j..j+3]. We update to W[j+4..j+7] only if all target indices <= limit, i.e., j+7 <= limit. Then compare targets and continue with j+=4.\nSo loop:\n```\nint j=0;\nwhile (j + 8 <= limit) { // because after updating we have windows up to j+7; next base=j+4 requires current max j+7 <= limit? Actually invariant at start: W[j..j+3] valid. To update and keep all four valid, need target indices j+4..j+7 <= limit => j+7<=limit.\n   w0 += c[j+s]-c[j];\n   ...\n   best=max(best,w0,...);\n   j+=4;\n}\n```\nAfter loop, we have current windows at positions j..j+3 (valid because invariant maintained? At start of each iteration yes; after last update and j+=4, new current indices are old j+4..old j+7 all <= limit due condition, so valid). Then handle remaining windows from j to limit sequentially:\n```\nfor (int t=j; t<=limit; ++t) {\n   if(t==j) use w[t-j]? But after loop we have W[j],W[j+1],W[j+2],W[j+3] in w[0..3]. Need compare them too maybe already compared when computed. We can just continue sliding one by one from current j:\n}\n```\nActually best should include all windows; during updates we compared target groups, and initial first four compared before loop. After final group update, those were compared. Then remaining indices > j+3? Since condition stopped because j+7>limit but maybe some of w[1..3] correspond to valid positions <= limit that haven't been compared? Let's trace.\n\nInitialize w0..w3 for indices 0..min(3,li